      "problem": "Calculate $\\int \\frac{4}{3}{y}^{7}\\,\\text{d}y.$",
      "worked_solution": "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4}{3}{y}^{7}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\frac{4}{3}\\int {y}^{7}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}(\\frac{{y}^{7+1}}{7+1})+C \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{8}}{8}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{8}}{6}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
